{"rephrased": "<think>\nWe are given the limit: \\(\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\sin(3x)}\\)\n\nFirst, I recall that as \\(x\\) approaches 0, the sine function has the property that \\(\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin(x)}{x} = 1\\). This suggests using a similar approach here by manipulating the expression to involve \\(\\frac{\\sin(kx)}{kx}\\) for some constant k.\n\nRewrite the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\sin(3x)} = \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{2x} \\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\sin(3x)} \\cdot \\frac{2x}{3x}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the extra factors:\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{2x} \\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\sin(3x)} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow take the limit as \\(x \\to 0\\):\n\nAs \\(x \\to 0\\),\n\n- \\(\\frac{\\sin(2x)}{2x} \\to 1\\) because if we let \\(u = 2x\\), then as \\(x \\to 0\\), \\(u \\to 0\\), and \\(\\lim_{u \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin u}{u} = 1\\).\n\n- Similarly, \\(\\frac{\\sin(3x)}{3x} \\to 1\\), so \\(\\frac{3x}{\\sin(3x)} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{\\sin(3x)}{3x}} \\to \\frac{1}{1} = 1\\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{2x} = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{3x}{\\sin(3x)} = 1\n\\]\n\nSo substituting,\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\sin(3x)} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left[ \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{2x} \\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\sin(3x)} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\right] = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, we can use L'H\u00f4pital's rule because as \\(x \\to 0\\), both numerator and denominator approach 0. So it is a 0/0 indeterminate form.\n\nApplying L'H\u00f4pital's rule:\n\nTake derivatives of numerator and denominator:\n\nNumerator: \\(\\sin(2x)\\) \u2192 derivative is \\(2 \\cos(2x)\\)\n\nDenominator: \\(\\sin(3x)\\) \u2192 derivative is \\(3 \\cos(3x)\\)\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\sin(3x)} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{2 \\cos(2x)}{3 \\cos(3x)}\n\\]\n\nNow plug in \\(x = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\cos(0)}{3 \\cos(0)} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nSame 