{0}^{\\infty} \\sin^2(u) \\cdot \\frac{2}{u^2} du\\).\n\nLet me calculate the coefficients carefully.\n\n\\(\\frac{\\sin^2(u)}{x^2} dx = \\frac{\\sin^2(u)}{(u/2)^2} \\cdot \\frac{du}{2} = \\frac{\\sin^2(u)}{u^2 / 4} \\cdot \\frac{du}{2} = \\sin^2(u) \\cdot \\frac{4}{u^2} \\cdot \\frac{du}{2} = \\sin^2(u) \\cdot \\frac{2}{u^2} du\\).\n\nSo, \\(2 \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\sin^2(u)}{u^2} du\\).\n\nI know that \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\sin^2(u)}{u^2} du = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nTherefore, the integral is \\(2 \\times \\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\pi\\).\n\nIs that right? Let me verify.\n\n\\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\sin^2(x)}{x^2} dx = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), yes.\n\nIn my case, u is dummy variable, so \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\sin^2(u)}{u^2} du = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), so multiplied by 2, yes, \\(\\pi\\).\n\nAnother way to think: Using the fact that \\(\\sin^2(2x) = \\frac{1 - \\cos(4x)}{2}\\).\n\nSo the integral is \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(4x)}{2x^2} dx\\).\n\nThat is, \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(4x)}{x^2} dx\\).\n\nI recall that integrals like \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(kx)}{x^2} dx\\) equal \\(\\frac{\\pi |k|}{2}\\).\n\nFor example, \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(kx)}{x^2} dx = \\frac{\\pi |k|}{2}\\).\n\nLet me confirm.\n\nYes, because \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(kx)}{x^2} dx = |k| \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(u)}{u^2} du\\), where u = |k|x.\n\nAnd \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(u)}{u^2} du = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nSince \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\sin(u)}{u} du = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), and integrating by parts, \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(u)}{u^2} du = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\sin(u)}{u} du = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nYes.\n\nSo in this case, \\(1 - \\cos(4x)\\), so k = 4.\n\nTherefore, \\(\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(4x)}{x^2} dx = \\frac{\\pi |4|}{2} = \\frac{4\\pi}{2} = 2\\pi\\).\n\nThen, my integral is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2\\pi = \\pi\\).\n\nSa