{"rephrased": "<think>\nWe are given a scenario where two players are betting on different outcomes in a sequence of dice rolls. Player A bets on rolling a 12 first, and Player B bets on rolling a 7 first. The dice are rolled repeatedly until either a 12 or a 7 occurs. I want to find the probability that Player A wins (i.e., that a 12 occurs before a 7).\n\nFirst, we recall that two fair dice are being rolled. The total number of possible outcomes when rolling two dice is 6 x 6 = 36.\n\nThe outcomes for a sum of 12:\n   There is only one outcome: (6,6). So, the probability of rolling a 12 in a single roll is P(12) = 1/36.\n\nThe outcomes for a sum of 7:\n   The possible outcomes are: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1). There are 6 outcomes. So, P(7) = 6/36 = 1/6.\n\nThe probability of neither (i.e., not 7 and not 12) in a single roll is:\n   P(neither) = 1 - P(12) - P(7) = 1 - 1/36 - 6/36 = (36 - 1 - 6)/36 = 29/36.\n\nThe game continues until either a 12 or a 7 appears. We can model this with a geometric-like distribution, but note that we are stopping at either event.\n\nLet P_A be the probability that a 12 occurs before a 7. We can write an equation conditioning on the first roll:\n\n   Case 1: The first roll is 12 -> then Player A wins. This happens with probability P(12) = 1/36.\n\n   Case 2: The first roll is 7 -> then Player A loses. This happens with probability P(7)=6/36.\n\n   Case 3: The first roll is neither -> then we are back to the original state. The probability of this happening is 29/36.\n\nSo, we have:\n   P_A = (1/36) * 1 + (6/36)*0 + (29/36)*P_A\n\nSolving for P_A:\n\n   P_A = 1/36 + (29/36)*P_A\n\n   P_A - (29/36)*P_A = 1/36\n\n   (7/36)*P_A = 1/36\n\n   P_A = (1/36) / (7/36) = (1/36) * (36/7) = 1/7.\n\nTherefore, the probability that Player A wins is 1/7.\n\nAlternatively, we can think about the odds. The probability that Player A wins should be proportional to the probability of rolling a 12 relative to the event of rolling either a 12 or a 7. Hence:\n\n   P_A = P(12) / [P(12) + P(7)] = (1/36) / (1/36 + 6/36) = (1/36) / (7/36) = 1/7.\n\nSo the answer is 1/7.\n\nHowever, let me double-check the events: The game ends when either a 12 or a 7 occurs. In the long run, the probability of stopping at a 12 versus stopping at a 7 is proportional to their respective probabilities.\n\nTherefore, we can also see it as:\n\n   P_A = P(12) / (P(12)+P(7)) = (1/36) / (7/36) = 1/7.\n\nHence, the probability that Player A wins is 1/7.\n</think>", "id": 52}
